Yes.

A word by itself is just a symbol. Life gives it referents, consequences, memory, emotion, use, and stakes. That lived contact is what turns the mark into meaning.

So meaning is not only:

\text{word} \rightarrow \text{definition}

but:

\text{word}
\rightarrow
\text{experience}
\rightarrow
\text{association}
\rightarrow
\text{action}
\rightarrow
\text{consequence}
\rightarrow
\text{updated meaning}

And then it loops back:

\text{life assigns meaning to words}
\leftrightarrow
\text{words reshape how life is perceived and acted upon}

That is why the same word can carry radically different weight for two people despite having the same dictionary entry.

The dictionary preserves the public invariant.

Life supplies the embodied instance.

Pantheon version:

Language stores the map; living supplies the terrain. Meaning is the delta between them, continually reconciled through use.